   "note": "Most stable site: fcc hollow (h = 1.05 A above top Cu layer). Site energies at 4x4x5 (vs O atom): fcc -4.846, hcp -4.784, top -3.077 eV; bridge relaxes to fcc. vs 1/2 O2(model): fcc -1.70 eV. Convergence: 2x2x4 (0.25 ML) -4.90, 3x3x4 -4.837, 3x3x6 -4.841, 4x4x5 -4.846 eV -> numerical/size error < 0.01 eV at low coverage; coverage effect ~0.06 eV going to 0.25 ML. Validation: (1) Cu a0 = 3.640 A vs experiment ~3.615 A (+0.7%, typical PBE). (2) Independent known result for the isolated-O reference: model O2 binding energy De = 2E(O) - E(O2) = 6.28 eV (d = 1.235 A), vs experiment De ~5.2 eV (D0 = 5.12 eV) but consistent with the known PBE value (~6.2 eV; Perdew-Burke-Ernzerhof 1996 O2 atomization 143 vs exp 120 kcal/mol). So the model reproduces its PBE training level, including PBE's ~1.1 eV O2 overbinding, i.e. the isolated-O-atom energy is ~0.5 eV too high per O relative to reality; the PBE-level adsorption energy vs atomic O is therefore likely too negative vs experiment by up to ~0.5 eV. Combining the model's E_ads(1/2 O2) = -1.70 eV with the experimental De/2 = 2.6 eV gives an 'experiment-corrected' estimate of ~ -4.3 eV. Uncertainty +/-0.5 eV covers this reference/functional error (dominant), MLIP-vs-DFT error, and coverage (0.06 eV); it does not include ZPE (~0.05-0.1 eV) or thermal effects. Value is a PBE-level (MLIP-surrogate) electronic adsorption energy at 0 K, not a directly measured quantity.",
